const books={
  luke:{name:'Luke',title:'The Cake That Quit',age:'Adult',audience:'Adults',cover:'assets/book-covers/luke-wide.png',imageBase:'assets/cake-book-adult-portrait',type:'Funny · written for adults',gift:true,giftFrom:'Someone who knows you too well',giftMessage:'For Luke. You said you wanted a quiet birthday.',identity:'Adult cartoon built from the uploaded photo and used throughout one complete story.',pages:[
    ['AN ENTIRELY REASONABLE PLAN','A quiet birthday. That was the idea.','Luke had rented the cottage because he wanted one birthday without notifications, small talk or anybody asking what he was doing next. He turned off his phone, opened a bottle of something decent and baked a strawberry cake. Pip the fox watched from the doorway with the solemn focus of an auditor. Luke placed the final berry on top. “There,” he said. “One thing today has gone exactly to plan.” The cake gave a small, unmistakable hiccup.'],
    ['THE PLAN RESIGNS','Then it grew legs.','Two pink boots appeared beneath the sponge. The cake hopped off its stand, landed neatly, and ran through the open door. Luke remained still for a moment, partly from shock and partly because he had spent four hours on the icing. Pip was already halfway down the path. “Of course,” Luke said, grabbing his jacket. “The cake has boundaries.” Then he followed, leaving his silent phone on the kitchen table.'],
    ['PUBLIC HUMILIATION','Lightly dusted.','The cake charged into the village bakery and slipped beneath a cooling rack. Luke did not. A ribbon of dough caught him across the chest while two bakers emptied bowls of flour in opposite directions. When the air cleared, Luke was white from eyebrows to trainers. Pip emerged looking equally powdered and far less embarrassed. Mrs Bell pointed a wooden spoon at the rear door. “Your dessert,” she said, “has ignored the one-way system.”'],
    ['THE FICTION SECTION','Things become less plausible.','In the bookshop, the cake squeezed behind a stack of atlases and left a pink handprint across Norway. Luke reached for it. The atlas snapped shut on his sleeve. Three hardbacks flapped off a shelf, circled his head and joined the cake on its way out. Mr Vale looked over his glasses. “I usually charge for damaged stock.” Luke freed himself. “Put it on the cake’s account.” Pip sneezed somewhere behind Travel.'],
    ['A BRIEF CAREER IN STREET THEATRE','The market gets involved.','The cake sprinted between market stalls, caught three falling oranges on its top tier and paused to bow. A crowd formed immediately. Someone began filming. Luke considered turning back, changing his name and moving abroad. Instead, he stepped over a basket of leeks and kept going. “Please don’t encourage it,” he called as the applause grew. The cake spun once, lost an orange, caught it again and ran on.'],
    ['THE BIT WITH THE BRIDGE','Nobody make this worse.','At the old stone bridge, the cake climbed onto the narrow wall. Six ducks gathered below with the professional patience of creatures expecting lunch. Luke slowed. The icing trembled in the breeze. Pip sat down and covered his eyes with both paws. “Nobody move,” Luke said. A tourist took a photograph. The cake tilted towards the water, recovered, then sprang to the opposite bank. The ducks complained loudly. Luke exhaled and began to suspect the escape had a destination.'],
    ['IT GATHERS A FOLLOWING','The chase acquires catering.','The cake cut through a woodland picnic. Rabbits abandoned their sandwiches. Two mice seized the napkins. A hedgehog joined the pursuit without asking for context, which Luke respected. By now the bakers, bookseller, market crowd and six indignant ducks were close behind. Luke had wanted to avoid people for the day. Instead, he appeared to be leading a parade with refreshments. Pip glanced back at the growing crowd and looked extremely pleased with himself.'],
    ['THE ACTUAL POINT','It was never escaping.','At sunset, the cake reached the empty village square and stopped beneath the lanterns. Luke arrived first, then everyone else tumbled in behind him. The cake turned slowly, as if checking attendance. That was when Luke understood. It had not run away from his birthday. It had collected one for him. “You could have left a note,” he said. The cake gave a proud wobble. Pip sat beside it, wearing the expression of somebody who had known all along.'],
    ['REPAIRS AND DELEGATION','Against all instinct, Luke accepts help.','The chase had cost the cake most of its icing, three strawberries and one pink boot. Mrs Bell produced cream. The market traders opened their berry baskets. The mice returned the missing boot, slightly chewed. Luke started to object, then stopped. He handed out spoons instead. Pip appointed himself Head Taster and found a paper hat to support the claim. For once, Luke let other people finish what he had started. The cake stood patiently in the middle of it all.'],
    ['A BETTER PLAN','Quiet was overrated.','Under the lanterns, Luke cut the first slice. There was cake for the bakers, bookseller, traders, tourists, rabbits, mice, hedgehog and six ducks who insisted they had saved the day. His phone remained unanswered on the cottage table. Nobody asked what came next. Pip licked cream from his nose. The cake gave one last, tiny hiccup and the square burst into laughter. Luke had planned a quiet birthday. He decided not to be precious about it.']
  ]},
  amara:{name:'Amara',title:'The Map Beneath the Moon',age:'6–8',audience:'Ages 6–8',cover:'assets/book-covers/amara-wide.png',imageBase:'assets/amara-book-portrait',type:'Adventure · ages 6–8',gift:false,giftFrom:'',giftMessage:'',identity:'One complete ten-page story, with language and illustrations shaped for ages 6–8.',pages:[
    ['A SILVER SECRET','A map under the rug.','At bedtime, Amara saw a silver glow beneath her rug. She pulled out a map. Moonlight ran across it like a bright river. At the window, a blue heron tapped its beak and pointed to one tiny boat.'],
    ['THE PAPER BOAT','A river appeared.','Amara folded the map into a paper boat. It grew just large enough for her to climb inside. The moonlit river slipped through the sleeping garden. The heron flew beside her, quiet as a shadow.'],
    ['THE WHISPERING WOOD','Follow the silver arrows.','The river entered a dark forest. Every path looked the same. Amara lifted the map to the moon. Silver arrows appeared on the trees. “This way,” she said, and the heron nodded.'],
    ['THREE STAR LOCKS','A door in the bank.','A stone door rose beside the river. It had three star-shaped locks. Amara studied the map and turned them from smallest to largest. Click, click, click. The old door opened.'],
    ['THE SKY LIBRARY','Books among the clouds.','Beyond the door floated a library in the clouds. Curved ladders reached through the sky. Glowing books travelled like tiny planets. Amara stepped from her boat and stared. She had never seen so many stories.'],
    ['THE EMPTY CASE','The North Star was gone.','An old owl showed Amara an empty glass case. “The North Star should shine here,” he said. Without it, travellers could lose their way. The silver map pointed towards a maze of moving shelves.'],
    ['THE BOOK MAZE','Three pictures, one path.','The shelves shifted around Amara. On the map were three pictures: a sun, a feather and a wave. She found each symbol in order. At once, the shelves opened to reveal a glowing staircase.'],
    ['THE STORM NEST','A star caught in shadow.','At the top of the tower, the North Star was tangled in dark storm threads. Wind pulled at Amara’s coat. She held the star steady while the heron loosened each knot with its long beak.'],
    ['LIGHT RETURNS','Every book woke up.','Amara carried the star back to its case. Golden light raced through every shelf and window. Books opened. Ladders danced. The old owl cheered. Far below, silver paths shone across the world.'],
    ['ONE LAST SPARK','Home before morning.','The paper boat carried Amara home before sunrise. In her room, it became an ordinary map again. Almost ordinary. One small point still glowed. Whenever Amara needed courage, the North Star would remember her.']
  ]},
  noah:{name:'Noah',title:'Noah and the Little Cloud',age:'3–5',audience:'Ages 3–5',cover:'assets/book-covers/noah-wide.png',imageBase:'assets/noah-book-portrait',type:'Gentle · ages 3–5',gift:true,giftFrom:'Mum and Dad',giftMessage:'For Noah, our little ray of sunshine.',identity:'One complete ten-page story, with short read-aloud sentences and simple repetition.',pages:[
    ['HELLO, CLOUD','A tiny cloud.','Noah found a tiny cloud behind a pot. “Hello,” said Noah. Drip! One little raindrop landed on his nose.'],
    ['DRIP, DROP','A little giggle.','The cloud made another drop. Drip! Noah giggled. The cloud wiggled. A red flower nearby looked very thirsty.'],
    ['THIRSTY FLOWERS','Drip, drop, plop.','Noah carried the cloud to the flowers. Drip, drop, plop! Up popped red. Up popped yellow. Up popped blue.'],
    ['A MUDDY DUCK','Quack, quack!','A little duck came down the path. It was brown with mud. “Oh, duck!” said Noah. “You need a wash.”'],
    ['SPLISH, SPLASH','A clean little duck.','The cloud made gentle rain. Splish, splash! Noah wiped the duck. Yellow feathers shone. “Quack!” said the happy duck.'],
    ['A RAINBOW','Red, yellow, blue.','The sun found the raindrops. A rainbow curved over the garden. Noah reached up. The cloud sparkled beside him.'],
    ['LOOKING UP','Where is your family?','The little cloud looked at the sky. Big clouds floated far away. “Is that your family?” Noah asked. The cloud gave a tiny nod.'],
    ['UP THE HILL','Step, step, step.','Noah walked up the grassy hill. Step, step, step. The duck followed. The little cloud floated close above his hands.'],
    ['HOME, LITTLE CLOUD','Together again.','At the top, the cloud family waited. The little cloud zoomed up. Drip, drop, hooray! Noah waved goodbye.'],
    ['GOODNIGHT','A cloud by the moon.','Noah slept snug in bed. Outside, the clouds made a moon shape. “Goodnight, little cloud,” Noah whispered.']
  ]}
};

const oliverPages=[
  ['THE FINAL STRAWBERRY','One suspicious hiccup.','Oliver had promised himself the cake would be finished before noon. He checked the icing, counted the candles and placed the last strawberry exactly in the centre. Pip sat beneath the table, pretending the cream did not interest him. Then the cake said, “Hic!” The strawberry jumped sideways. Oliver stared at it. Pip’s ears rose. A second hiccup shook sugar across the table. Oliver reached for the cake stand, but the sponge shifted away from his hands.'],
  ['TWO PINK BOOTS','The evidence runs away.','Before Oliver could call for help, two icing-covered boots pushed through the bottom layer. The cake hopped down, tested its balance and sprinted through the open door. “That took me all morning!” Oliver shouted. Pip shot after it. Oliver grabbed his coat and followed, trying to decide which was worse: losing his birthday cake or explaining that it had escaped under its own power. At the garden gate, the cake looked back, hiccupped once and vanished into the lane.'],
  ['THE BAKERY','A flour-covered witness.','The cake burst into Mrs Bell’s bakery during the lunch rush. It ducked beneath a tray of rolls. Oliver did not. Dough wrapped around his shoulders and a bowl of flour tipped over his head. By the time he stood up, Pip had become a white fox and three customers were applauding. Mrs Bell pointed towards the rear door with a wooden spoon. “It went that way,” she said. “And if it steals my oven, I’m sending you the bill.”'],
  ['THE BOOKSHOP','Travel causes trouble.','In the bookshop, pink icing led Oliver to a stack of atlases. The cake was hiding behind Europe. He moved quietly, reached between two shelves and almost caught it. An atlas snapped shut on his sleeve. The cake wriggled free, sending hardbacks flapping into the air like startled birds. Pip sneezed beneath Travel. Mr Vale peered over the counter. “Running cakes belong in Fiction,” he said. Oliver freed his arm. “At the moment, it belongs to nobody.”'],
  ['THE MARKET','An audience makes it worse.','By the time Oliver reached the market, half the village had noticed the chase. The cake bounced between vegetable stalls, clipped an orange display and caught three falling oranges on its top tier. It stopped, turned and bowed. The crowd cheered. “Please don’t encourage it,” Oliver called, though he was laughing despite himself. The cake spun again, lost one orange, caught it on the rebound and raced towards the river. Pip glanced at Oliver as if to say the cake was getting ideas.'],
  ['THE BRIDGE','A choice with ducks below.','The cake climbed onto the narrow wall of the old bridge. Six ducks gathered beneath it with open beaks. Oliver could rush forward and risk knocking it into the water, or wait and risk losing it again. He chose patience. One slow step. Then another. The cake leaned towards the river. Pip covered his eyes with both paws. At the last second it sprang to the far bank, scattering icing but staying upright. The ducks complained. Oliver followed, now certain the cake had somewhere to be.'],
  ['THE PICNIC','The chase becomes a parade.','Beyond the bridge, the cake tore through a woodland picnic. Rabbits dropped their sandwiches. Two mice seized a napkin and ran after it. A hedgehog joined without asking why. Oliver could hear the bakers, bookseller and market crowd close behind. What had begun as a private disaster had become a noisy procession. “Nearly got you!” he called. The cake glanced back and wiggled its cherries. Pip barked once, delighted. Oliver noticed that the cake always slowed until everyone caught up.'],
  ['THE SQUARE','The clue Oliver nearly missed.','At sunset, the cake reached the empty village square and stopped beneath the lanterns. Oliver skidded to a halt beside it. Mrs Bell arrived, followed by Mr Vale, the traders, rabbits, mice, one hedgehog and six deeply offended ducks. The cake turned as if checking a list. Suddenly the slowing down, the detours and the ridiculous bow made sense. “You weren’t trying to escape,” Oliver said. “You were collecting guests.” The cake gave a proud wobble. Pip looked unbearably smug.'],
  ['THE REPAIR CREW','Everyone brings something.','The journey had cost the cake most of its icing, three strawberries and one pink boot. Oliver looked at the damage, then at the crowd it had gathered. “We can fix this,” he said. Mrs Bell brought cream. The market traders opened their berry baskets. The mice returned the missing boot, slightly nibbled. Mr Vale folded paper hats from an old catalogue. Pip claimed the tallest one and appointed himself Head Taster. Oliver stopped trying to control every detail and handed out spoons.'],
  ['THE LAST HICCUP','Better than perfect.','Under the lanterns, Oliver cut the first slice. There was cake for the bakers, booksellers, traders, rabbits, mice, hedgehog and six ducks who insisted on equal portions. Pip licked cream from his nose. Around the square, people who had never spoken before traded stories about the chase. The cake gave one final, tiny “Hic!” Everyone laughed. Oliver had wanted a perfect birthday cake. What he had instead was lopsided, slightly muddy and surrounded by friends. He did not miss perfect at all.']
];
books.oliver={...books.luke,name:'Oliver',title:'Oliver and the Cake That Wouldn’t Wait',age:'9–12',audience:'Ages 9–12',cover:'assets/book-covers/oliver-wide.png',imageBase:'assets/cake-book-portrait',type:'Funny · ages 9–12',gift:false,giftFrom:'',giftMessage:'',identity:'One complete ten-page story, with a layered plot and more detailed writing.',pages:oliverPages};

